<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isra' and Mi'raj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Night Journey and Ascension, Ascension of Muhammad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isra' and Mi'raj, the Night Travel and Ascension, are Islamic narratives of a night journey in which Muhammad ascends to the heavens, sees the afterlife and returns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> has only brief references, in verses of Surah an-Najm and in the 17th chapter, later called Surah al-Isra'; the detailed accounts come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> compiled after his lifetime. In the fuller versions he is accompanied by the angels Gabriel and Michael, meets earlier prophets, and reaches the Sidrat al-Muntaha, where the fifty daily prayers required of him are reduced to five. Classical commentaries mostly read the journey as bodily, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> generally as spiritual, and the 27th night of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> is widely observed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isra-and-miraj.jpg" alt="Isra' and Mi'raj" width="330" height="114" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: عامر شاہین · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:017001_Bani-Israel_UrduScript.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Miguel Asín Palacios argued that Dante drew on Ibn Arabi and on accounts of Muhammad's night journey, the <b>Isra and Mi'raj</b>, known in Latin as the Liber scalae Machometi; Francesco Gabrieli doubted it for lack of a means of transmission, while Maria Corti speculated that Brunetto Latini could…</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside, the Ottoman tilework carries Surah Ya-Sin, with Surah 17, Al-Isra', which tells of the <b>Night Journey</b>, inscribed above it.</blockquote><a href="/work/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
